    {
      "id": "lpic1-service-client-targets",
      "version": 1,
      "track": "LPIC-1",
      "topic": "Service names and transport",
      "unit": "linux-service-names",
      "title": "Connect the intended service, not just an open port",
      "summary": "Compare name lookups with supplied listener facts and repair three virtual clients.",
      "objective": "Configure lookup to reach the supplied resolver, report-client to reach reports, and admin to reach remote. Use the supplied transport and destination for each role; names, aliases or decimal ports are accepted. Keep the reference file unchanged and pass both available-listener and unavailable-listener cases.",
      "retry_guidance": "Read the required destination and actual protocol/port beside the failing case. Check the saved name mapping, correct only the relevant field or record, then retry.",
      "difficulty": "Beginner",
      "minutes": 15,
      "steps": [
        "Inspect the supplied name database and listener facts",
        "Repair the client settings",
        "Check destination, delivery and unavailable-listener behavior"
      ],
      "guide": [],
      "explanation": "A service name resolves to a protocol-specific port; it does not choose a server or start a process. The supplied server accepts resolver requests only on UDP 53, reports on TCP 8080 and remote access on TCP 22. DNS can use TCP as well as UDP in real systems; this fixture deliberately supplies only the UDP listener. A successful lookup is different from reaching the intended listener, and an unavailable listener can coexist with a correct mapping.",
      "hints": [
        "Run getent services domain/tcp and getent services domain/udp. Both names exist, but this fixture supplies only one resolver listener.",
        "A custom service can use a nondefault port: compare reports-demo with the supplied reports listener.",
        "Choose TCP or UDP separately from the service field. Names are case-sensitive; a decimal port bypasses name lookup."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://www.lpi.org/our-certifications/exam-101-102-objectives/",
        "https://www.sourceware.org/glibc/manual/latest/html_node/Services-Database.html",
        "https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/services.5.html",
        "https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/getent.1.html"
      ],
      "simplifications": "Supplied virtual /etc/services only, not the host NSS database. Bounded case-sensitive ASCII names, distinct per-protocol names/ports, TCP/UDP only; unsupported records and shell syntax are diagnosed. The read-only terminal supports cat /etc/services and literal getent services queries; help is a simulator helper. Source-held IPv4 listeners and finite opaque TCP/UDP payload delivery use the existing transport reducer. No real sockets, process lookup, network, application messages, DNS resolution, TLS, retransmission, timing or service lifecycle. A successful lookup or payload is not application interoperability. Port assignments shown are a limited subset, not complete LPIC-1 objective coverage.",
