      "id": "lpic1-services-file-repair",
      "version": 1,
      "track": "LPIC-1",
      "topic": "Service names and transport",
      "unit": "linux-service-names",
      "title": "Repair service names without moving the server",
      "summary": "Correct a misleading name database while preserving unrelated service meanings.",
      "objective": "Repair the virtual /etc/services mappings so admin reaches remote and report-client reaches reports. Keep all other supplied canonical names and aliases resolving to their original protocol and port. Save the file and verify both available-listener and unavailable-listener cases; leave client settings unchanged.",
      "retry_guidance": "Read the required destination and actual protocol/port beside the failing case. Check the saved name mapping, correct only the relevant field or record, then retry.",
      "difficulty": "Intermediate",
      "minutes": 18,
      "steps": [
        "Inspect the supplied name database and listener facts",
        "Repair and save the virtual services file",
        "Check destination, delivery and unavailable-listener behavior"
      ],
      "guide": [],
      "explanation": "Both wrong mappings point at other open TCP ports. A payload can therefore arrive at the wrong destination. Repairing ssh/secure-shell to 22 and reports-demo/reports to 8080 fixes name resolution while preserving unrelated entries. Saving the virtual file changes the next lookup; it does not move, start or restart a server. Formatting, comments and record order need not match one sample solution.",
      "hints": [
        "Run getent services ssh/tcp and getent services reports/tcp; compare each with the supplied destination role.",
        "Correct the port/protocol fields in the virtual editor, preserving names and aliases. Save before running a new lookup.",
        "Reaching any open port is insufficient. Check that each client selects its required role and that unrelated names retain their meanings."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://www.lpi.org/our-certifications/exam-101-102-objectives/",
        "https://www.sourceware.org/glibc/manual/latest/html_node/Services-Database.html",
        "https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/services.5.html",
        "https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/getent.1.html"
      ],
      "simplifications": "Supplied virtual /etc/services only, not the host NSS database. Bounded case-sensitive ASCII names, distinct per-protocol names/ports, TCP/UDP only; unsupported records and shell syntax are diagnosed. The read-only terminal supports cat /etc/services and literal getent services queries; help is a simulator helper. Source-held IPv4 listeners and finite opaque TCP/UDP payload delivery use the existing transport reducer. No real sockets, process lookup, network, application messages, DNS resolution, TLS, retransmission, timing or service lifecycle. A successful lookup or payload is not application interoperability. Port assignments shown are a limited subset, not complete LPIC-1 objective coverage.",
